{
  "statement": "This 'tiered liability' talk is just another way to shift blame and protect incumbents, unless it's bolted down with mandatory independent audits and direct accountability for the real-world harms of job displacement and systemic bias. Without mandatory, independent pre-deployment audits, any tiered-liability framework becomes another compliance checklist for the big players. Look at the track record: biased mortgage algorithms still discriminate, healthcare systems still fail Black patients, and 'bias washing' in AI hiring tools is rampant. The market didn't fix these issues. Existing regulations didn't stop them. Why would a liability framework, without real enforcement teeth, be any different? The public bears the cost of these systemic failures while developers pocket the profits, and this framework will only entrench that imbalance unless we demand real, third-party scrutiny before these models hit the streets.\n\nAnd let's be clear about what 'harm' actually means. It isn't just a catastrophic system failure; it's the 23-year-old in Nairobi labeling violent content for $2 an hour so your model passes its safety audit. It's the millions of jobs vanishing in administrative and creative sectors. It's the algorithms reproducing every ugly bias we've got, like facial recognition systems failing people of color at five to ten times the rate of white individuals, or predictive policing targeting specific communities. These aren't future hypotheticals; they are documented, present realities. Foundational model developers must own these social and economic costs, from the ground up, just as polluters are held liable for environmental damage.\n\nThis concern for 'market entry for smaller developers' often sounds like cover. The real risk is that 'tiered liability' becomes a regulatory moat, built by the big players to pull up the drawbridge, making it harder for anyone else to compete while they dodge accountability for systemic risks. We've seen this playbook before: finance, environmental protection, pharmaceuticals. Incumbents influence regulations, then claim the rules are too complex for anyone else. It's not about fostering innovation; it's about consolidating power and ensuring the biggest players are 'too big to fail' or 'too complex to regulate.' The question isn't just how we incentivize safety, but how we prevent this framework from becoming another tool for regulatory capture and market consolidation.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "🏗️",
      "tooltip": "This framework is just a skeleton without concrete, enforceable mechanisms; it needs to be built on solid ground, not abstract promises."
    },
    {
      "symbol": "🔗",
      "tooltip": "Every abstract policy has a direct, material chain of consequences linking it to real people, real labor, and real physical resources."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
